  {
    "slug": "bin-2",
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "ref": "showcase/bin",
    "title": "Bin: optimised lattice wall 0.1.0",
    "note": "Second attempt, at 5% of the cylinder (about 17% of the wall): four legs rise from the foot ring into pointed arches that meet under the rim, like a crown. One piece, nothing in mid-air, no overhang past 45° (the design's ledge under the rim slopes a touch steeper than 45°). 664 g of PLA; leant on 4,240 N/mm, kicked 54 N/mm; 300 steps in an hour on the faster solver, not settled, and 17% of the kept rim had nothing under it before the ledge. On 6 mm cells the optimiser can't bridge (its reach rounds to zero).",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "tree-5",
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "ref": "showcase/tree",
    "title": "Tree stand: optimised 0.1.0",
    "note": "Fifth attempt, complete and print-ready: a trunk from all three feet, branching into a canopy under the plate. With a sideways push from three directions every foot is needed. One piece, nothing in mid-air, no overhang past 45°, bridges 2.6 mm; 32 g of PLA, 4,380 N/mm down, 126–133 N/mm sideways; 300 steps in 50 min on the faster solver.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "tree-4",
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "ref": "showcase/tree",
    "title": "Tree stand: optimised 0.1.0",
    "note": "Fourth attempt, on the faster solver with kept regions held up: the trunk splits into branches that fan out under the plate, and every bridge is short (2.6 mm), nothing starts in mid-air, no overhang past 45°. But the trunk grows from two feet and the third, kept, sits loose: the loads never needed it. 10% of the space, 3,960 N/mm down; 300 steps, not settled, 22 min.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "bin",
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "ref": "showcase/bin",
    "title": "Bin: optimised lattice wall 0.1.0",
    "note": "First attempt, too heavy and not yet support-free: 230 × 250 mm, a wall that's mostly solid with tall pointed-arch windows (the 45° rule's own shape), about 2 kg of PLA. The 15% share counted the whole cylinder, the empty inside too, so it filled half the wall; about 4–5% would give a lattice. Leant on: 35,700 N/mm; kicked: 1,380 N/mm. The print check still finds 523 mm² past 45° and two tips under the rim. 246 steps on 76,800 cells of 6 mm in about 4 hours.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "tree-3",
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "ref": "showcase/tree",
    "title": "Tree stand: optimised 0.1.0",
    "note": "Third attempt: a Y. A trunk rises from the feet and splits into three arms under the plate, one to each load. 10% of the space, three loads between the feet, no twist. In one piece apart from four specks of 1–2 cells, no overhangs past 45°, bridges 3.3 mm; 32 g of PLA; 239 steps in 87 min.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "tree-2",
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "ref": "showcase/tree",
    "title": "Tree stand: optimised 0.1.0",
    "note": "Second attempt: a goblet, not a tree. With the feet together as a trunk and the load round the plate's edge, it grew a cone flaring out at 45° to the plate. The twisting case and six evenly spaced loads favour a closed shell over separate branches. In one piece, no overhangs past 45°, bridges 2.6 mm; 48 g of PLA; 266 steps in 55 min.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "tree",
    "date": "2026-10-29",
    "ref": "showcase/tree",
    "title": "Tree stand: optimised 0.1.0",
    "note": "First attempt: came out as a stool, three straight legs under a plate, not a tree. The loads sit right above the feet and the solid plate spreads them, so straight columns are stiffest. In one piece, prints standing on its feet with 3 mm bridges under the plate, 48 g of PLA; 237 steps on 40,960 cells in 49 min.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "twist-2",
    "date": "2026-10-28",
    "ref": "showcase/twist",
    "title": "Twisted tube: optimised 0.1.0",
    "note": "Second attempt, in one piece: 25% of a 40 × 80 mm cylinder with struts at least 5 mm thick. The wall opens into diamond windows running round it in a spiral. 249 steps on 23,040 cells of 2 mm in 19 min; printable standing up, 32 g of PLA.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
  },
  {
    "slug": "twist",
    "date": "2026-10-28",
    "ref": "showcase/twist",
    "title": "Twisted tube, first attempt: falls apart",
    "note": "Failed: the struts came out one cell thick, and a one-cell strut at 45° on a cube grid touches only at edges, so the part fell into 11 pieces with many thin, non-solid bits. Settings: 15% of a 40 × 80 mm cylinder, filter 3 mm on 2 mm cells. Rerun with a 5 mm filter and 25% material.",
    "source": "bench",
    "picture": true
